<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Nassau</h1><p class="st-meta">1160 CE – 1255 CE (approx.) · capital Nassau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The county on the Lahn whose division in 1255 produced the Walram and Otto lines of Nassau, ancestors of the royal houses of the Netherlands and Luxembourg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Laurenburg, who held lands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>, built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nassau-castle">Nassau Castle</a> around 1125 on land claimed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishopric-of-worms">Bishopric of Worms</a>. Around 1160 they began to call themselves counts of Nassau. They extended their territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westerwald">Westerwald</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegerland">Siegerland</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county lay in hilly, wooded country with mining and iron working, especially around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>. Its counts were mostly modest regional lords, often in dispute with the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. Its Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walram-ii-count-of-nassau">Walram II</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a> jointly ruled it in the mid 13th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1255 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walram-ii-count-of-nassau">Walram II</a> and Otto I divided Nassau along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>. Walram's line produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-of-nassau">King Adolf of Nassau</a>, elected in 1292, and later the grand dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>; Otto's line produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> and the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">Orange-Nassau</a>, today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> royal family.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-nassau.jpg" alt="County of Nassau" width="250" height="272"><figcaption>Image: William Robert Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nassau1547.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1160 CE – 1255 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nassau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Walram II of Nassau, Otto I of Nassau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hessian and Moselle Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nassau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1208&amp;focus=county-of-nassau" aria-label="County of Nassau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4858 429 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Nassau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5178 685L5229 694M5178 685L5188 680M5178 685L5189 673"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5188 680h0M5189 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5178 685h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5178 685h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nassau. <a href="/atlas?year=1208&amp;focus=county-of-nassau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
